---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title       = 'Are Ravens Smarter Than Crows?';
const description = 'Ravens and crows both rank among the most intelligent birds. This page compares their cognitive abilities directly with sourced data from controlled experiments.';
const path        = '/questions/raven-vs-crow-intelligence/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',      url: '/' },
  { name: 'Questions', url: '/questions/' },
  { name: 'Are Ravens Smarter Than Crows?', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Which is smarter — a raven or a crow?', a: 'Both Common Ravens and American/Carrion Crows perform at similar high levels on most cognitive tasks. Ravens have shown slightly more flexible planning in Kabadayi & Osvath (2017); crows (particularly New Caledonian Crows) show more sophisticated habitual tool use. For Common Crows vs Common Ravens, the difference is small and task-dependent.' },
  { q: 'Are New Caledonian Crows smarter than ravens?', a: 'New Caledonian Crows (Corvus moneduloides) excel at tool manufacture and use — they make and modify tools in the wild, which Common Ravens do not. On planning and social cognition tasks, ravens and New Caledonian Crows perform comparably. They are differently specialised, not simply ranked.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} ogType="article"
  datePublished="2026-11-07" dateModified="2026-11-07"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} faq={faq} pageType="Article">

<article class="qa-page">
  <nav class="qa-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <ol>
      {breadcrumbs.map((b, i) => (
        <li>
          {i < breadcrumbs.length - 1
            ? <><a href={b.url}>{b.name}</a><span aria-hidden="true"> › </span></>
            : <span aria-current="page">{b.name}</span>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </nav>
  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Question · Biology</p>
    <h1>Are Ravens Smarter Than Crows?</h1>
  </header>
  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens and crows are both exceptional — they perform at similar high levels across most cognitive tasks tested in controlled experiments. Ravens showed slightly stronger performance in a 2017 planning study (Kabadayi & Osvath); New Caledonian Crows excel at tool use. For the species most likely to be compared — Common Raven vs American Crow or Carrion Crow — the cognitive difference is small and task-dependent. Neither is simply "smarter".</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What They Have in Common</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> All corvids in the raven/crow group share:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Large brain-to-body ratio — among the highest of any birds.</li>
      <li>Vocal learning — calls are learned, not fully innate.</li>
      <li>Social complexity — long-term relationships, dominance hierarchies, cooperation.</li>
      <li>Food caching with spatial memory and cache protection.</li>
      <li>Apparent understanding of causal relationships in physical tasks.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Where Ravens Edge Ahead</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Kabadayi and Osvath's 2017 planning study used the same protocol on ravens and great apes. When the same test has been run with crows (American or Carrion), ravens have shown slightly more flexible performance — though the studies are not always directly comparable in design.<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <p>Ravens also show more complex social cognition around cache protection — particularly the ability to infer what a potential thief can see based on the thief's position relative to opaque barriers.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Where Crows Edge Ahead</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> New Caledonian Crows (<em>Corvus moneduloides</em>) are the tool-use champions of the corvid family. They manufacture and modify tools — including hooks from plant stems — in the wild, something Common Ravens do not do habitually. In sequential tool-use tasks requiring multiple steps, New Caledonian Crows outperform ravens.<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
    <p>American and Carrion Crows also perform very well on many cognitive tasks; the available data does not clearly rank them below Common Ravens overall.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Why Ranking Species Intelligence Is Problematic</h2>
    <p>Intelligence is not a single dimension. Different selective pressures produce different cognitive specialisations. Ravens evolved in social environments requiring advanced social cognition; New Caledonian Crows evolved in environments where tool use was rewarding. Comparing them is like asking whether a mathematician is smarter than a musician.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/questions/how-smart-are-ravens/">How smart are ravens?</a></li>
      <li><a href="/compare/raven-vs-crow/">Raven vs crow — field identification</a></li>
      <li><a href="/behaviour/raven-intelligence/">Raven Intelligence — full chapter</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-faq qa-section" aria-labelledby="faq-h2">
    <h2 id="faq-h2">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    {faq.map(item => (
      <details class="qa-faq-item">
        <summary class="qa-faq-q">{item.q}</summary>
        <p class="qa-faq-a">{item.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="qa-sources qa-section" aria-labelledby="src-h2">
    <h2 id="src-h2">Sources</h2>
    <ol class="qa-src-list">
      <li id="src-1">Heinrich, B. (2000). <em>Mind of the Raven</em>. HarperCollins. — corvid cognition overview.</li>
      <li id="src-2">Kabadayi, C. & Osvath, M. (2017). Ravens parallel great apes in flexible planning. <em>Science</em> 357, 202–204. — planning comparison.</li>
      <li id="src-3">Taylor, A.H. et al. (2010). New Caledonian crows reason about hidden causal agents. <em>PNAS</em> 107(37), 16381–86. — tool use and causal reasoning in NCC.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="qa-claim-note"><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> peer-reviewed study or field-guide data</p>
  </section>
</article>
</BaseLayout>

